@startuml resolve-and-reopen
title US-13: Resolve and Reopen - Resolved Alert Does Not Block New Detection

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

actor Operator
participant "Express API" as API
database "TimescaleDB\nalerts table" as TSDB
participant "Alert\nEvaluator\n(leader)" as Evaluator
queue "Kafka\nalerts" as Kafka

== Operator Resolves Alert ==

Operator -> API : PATCH /alerts/VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700000000000\n{ status: RESOLVED }

API -> TSDB : UPDATE alerts SET status = 'RESOLVED',\nresolved_at = now()\nWHERE alert_id = 'VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700000000000'

note over TSDB
  Record stays in the table permanently.
  This is the audit trail - never deleted.
  Resolved alerts are queryable for history.
end note

TSDB --> API : updated
API --> Operator : 200 OK

== Anomaly Recurs Later ==

note over Evaluator
  VesselB goes dark again in a new time window.
  Alert Evaluator does not read the alerts table.
  It evaluates based on last_seen_ms in the Redis hash
  and the alert-state:{entity_id} suppression key.
end note

Evaluator -> Kafka : publish new alert event\n{ alert_id: VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700005000000,\n  entity_id: VesselB,\n  alert_type: SIGNAL_LOSS,\n  window_start_ms: 1700005000000 }

note over Kafka
  New window_start_ms = new alert_id.
  Completely independent of the resolved alert.
  No reopen logic - just a fresh record.
end note

Kafka -> API : consume new alert event
API -> TSDB : INSERT INTO alerts ( alert_id: VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700005000000, status: NEW, ... )\nON CONFLICT DO NOTHING

note over TSDB
  Two separate rows now exist:
  VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700000000000 -> RESOLVED
  VesselB:SIGNAL_LOSS:1700005000000 -> NEW
  Full history preserved.
end note

@enduml
